---
title: Phalacrocoracidae
---
# Phalacrocoracidae

Phalacrocoracidae is the cormorant and shag family, some forty species of
long-bodied, long-necked seabirds with a hooked bill. They propel themselves
after prey well beneath the surface, driven by the feet, and can remain down for
more than a minute at a time. The plumage is deliberately wettable, which
reduces buoyancy and makes the descent cheap; the cost is the spread-wing posture
adopted afterwards to dry out. Colonies are noisy and conspicuous, on cliff
ledges, in dead trees or on artificial structures. See [[great-cormorant]].
